    "text": "# `merge_intervals` \u2014 Merging Overlapping Closed Integer Intervals\n\n```python\ndef merge_intervals(intervals):\n    \"\"\"\n    Merge overlapping closed integer intervals.\n\n    Args:\n        intervals: iterable of (start, end) pairs, inclusive on both ends.\n\n    Returns:\n        A new sorted list of disjoint, non-adjacent (start, end) tuples.\n        The input is not mutated.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not intervals:\n        return []\n\n    # Copy + sort so we never touch the caller's data.\n    ordered = sorted((tuple(iv) for iv in intervals), key=lambda iv: (iv[0], iv[1]))\n\n    merged = [ordered[0]]\n    for start, end in ordered[1:]:\n        last_start, last_end = merged[-1]\n        # Closed intervals: [1,3] and [4,5] are adjacent but NOT overlapping.\n        # They only merge when start <= last_end (i.e. they share a point or overlap).\n        if start <= last_end:\n            merged[-1] = (last_start, max(last_end, end))\n        else:\n            merged.append((start, end))\n\n    return merged\n```\n\n## The Invariant\n\nAt the top of each loop iteration, `merged` satisfies:\n\n> **`merged` is a list of disjoint, non-adjacent intervals, sorted by start, and it is exactly the merge of every interval already consumed from `ordered`.**\n\nTwo consequences make the algorithm correct:\n\n1. **Disjointness + sortedness**",
